==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
Of {{onomatopoeic|en|nocap=1}} origin. Compare {{cog|stq|klatskje|t=to smack, slap}}, {{cog|fy|kletse}}, {{m|fy|kletskje}}, {{cog|nl|kletsen|t=to smack, slap, clash}}, {{cog|nds-de|klattsen}}, {{m|nds-de|klatsken|t=to smack, splash}}, {{cog|de|klatschen|t=to clap, smack, slap}} and {{m|de|Klatsch|t=a clapping sound; the din resulting from two or more things colliding}}, {{cog|da|klaske|t=to clash, splatter}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklæʃ/|[ˈkʰl̥æʃ]|a=RP,GA,AU}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-clash.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklaʃ/|a=SSB,Northern England,Scotland,Wales}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɛʃ/|a=NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|æʃ|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|clash}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|onomatopoeia}} A [[loud]] sound, like the crashing together of metal objects; a crash.
#: {{ux|en|I heard a '''clash''' from the kitchen, and rushed in to find the cat had knocked over some pots and pans.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2025-01-28|author=Patricia McLinn|title=Lattimore Mountain (The Sheila Mackey Collection, Book 1)|publisher=Craig Place Books|isbn=9781960714237
|text=Alone amid '''clashes''' of rain against the house.}}
# A [[skirmish]], a hostile encounter.
#* {{RQ:Time|title=Who Is Balendra Shah, the Rapper On Track to Become Nepal’s Next Prime Minister|date=March 11 2026|author=Chad de Guzman
|text=Protesters took to the streets and stormed federal buildings. At least 77 people died in violent '''clashes''' between protesters and police and then-Prime Minister K.P. Sharma Oli was forced to resign.
|url=https://time.com/article/2026/03/11/balendra-shah-elections-nepal-prime-minister-rsp-gen-z-protests/
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260311233009/https://time.com/article/2026/03/11/balendra-shah-elections-nepal-prime-minister-rsp-gen-z-protests/}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} [[match]]; a game between two sides.
# An angry [[argument]].
# Opposition; contradiction; such as between differing or contending interests, views, purposes etc.
#: {{coi|en|'''clash''' of beliefs}}
#: {{coi|en|culture '''clash'''}}
# A [[combination]] of garments that do not look good together, especially because of conflicting colours.
#: {{ux|en|She was wearing a horrible '''clash''' of red and orange.}}
# {{lb|en|hurling}} An instance of restarting the game after a "dead ball", where it is dropped between two opposing players, who can fight for possession.
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} [[chatter|Chatter]]; [[gossip]]; [[idle]] [[talk]].
# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal|possibly|obsolete}}<!--EDD: Cum., Wm., Yks. --> A [[heavy]] [[fall]] (of [[rain]]); heavy rain.
#: {{syn|en|blash}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1802|title=The Farmer's Magazine: A Periodical Work, Exclusively Devoted to Agriculture, and Rural Affairs ...|page=172
|text=clay soils ploughed in Autumn must often meet with heavy '''clashes''' of rain before frost: therefore , those salutary effects, expected to be derived from a frosty Winter, must often be frustrated, by the land being too much condensed by the rains before the frost comes on.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=Thomas Clarke|title=Specimens of the dialects of Westmorland, by T. Clarke|page=39
|text=T' taties offer't ta be a grand crop et forend, bet then seea mitch '''clash''' an caald wes again em, an thaev tean up varra smo, {{...}} }}

